// =============================================================================
// Token attribution — who spent what, on which model, for which cost centre
// =============================================================================
// This is the query that answers the question Providence's finance and platform
// teams will ask within a month of go-live and cannot answer today:
//
//     "Foundry billed us $X. Which teams, and which applications, caused it?"
//
// Azure Cost Management can tell you what a Foundry deployment cost. It cannot
// tell you that 62% of it was the revenue-cycle denial-summarisation batch job,
// because from Azure's point of view there is one resource and one meter. The
// dimensions below exist only because the gateway stamped them on every call.
//
// SCOPE: Log Analytics workspace log-provdemo-...
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHICH TABLE, AND WHY THIS TRIPS PEOPLE UP
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Nearly every sample you will find online — including Microsoft's own — uses
//
//     customMetrics | extend Team = tostring(customDimensions["Team"])
//
// That is the CLASSIC Application Insights schema. Workspace-based App Insights
// resources — the default now, and what this platform uses — store the same
// telemetry in the Log Analytics workspace under different table and column
// names:
//
//     classic                    workspace-based
//     ------------------------   ------------------------
//     customMetrics              AppMetrics
//     customDimensions           Properties
//     requests                   AppRequests
//     traces                     AppTraces
//     dependencies               AppDependencies
//     exceptions                 AppExceptions
//     valueSum                   Sum
//     timestamp                  TimeGenerated
//
// Querying customMetrics from the Log Analytics blade returns an empty result
// with no error, which reads exactly like "the metric policy isn't working".
// It is working — you are reading the wrong table. This cost a debugging cycle
// while building this session, and it is the single most likely reason a
// Providence engineer will conclude token metrics are broken.
//
// (The classic aliases DO still resolve inside the Application Insights > Logs
// blade, which is why the same query appears to work for one person and not
// another. Prefer the workspace names: they work from both places.)
//
// PREREQUISITE that silently breaks everything below: the APIM diagnostic must
// have metrics = true. See Session 1/infra/02-ai-gateway.bicep. Without it
// these tables are empty and, again, nothing errors.
// =============================================================================

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. Spend by cost centre and team — the chargeback report
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
AppMetrics
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| where Name in ("Total Tokens", "Prompt Tokens", "Completion Tokens")
| extend
    CostCenter  = tostring(Properties.CostCenter),
    Team        = tostring(Properties.Team),
    App         = tostring(Properties.App),
    Model       = tostring(Properties.Model),
    ServiceTier = tostring(Properties.ServiceTier),
    ApiId       = tostring(Properties.ApiId)
| summarize Tokens = sum(Sum) by CostCenter, Team, Model, Name
| evaluate pivot(Name, sum(Tokens))
| order by CostCenter asc, Team asc

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. Daily trend per team — the capacity conversation
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Run separately. Growth rate matters more than the absolute number: a team
// doubling week over week needs a PTU conversation before it hits the quota,
// not after.
//
// AppMetrics
// | where TimeGenerated > ago(30d)
// | where Name == "Total Tokens"
// | extend Team = tostring(Properties.Team)
// | summarize Tokens = sum(Sum) by bin(TimeGenerated, 1d), Team
// | render timechart

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. Prompt-to-completion ratio by application — the optimisation signal
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A very high prompt:completion ratio usually means an application is stuffing
// the same static context into every call. That is the cheapest cost saving
// available and it needs no model change: cache it, or move it to a system
// message the provider can cache. This query finds those applications without
// anybody having to volunteer that they exist.
//
// AppMetrics
// | where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
// | where Name in ("Prompt Tokens", "Completion Tokens")
// | extend App = tostring(Properties.App), Team = tostring(Properties.Team)
// | summarize Tokens = sum(Sum) by App, Team, Name
// | evaluate pivot(Name, sum(Tokens))
// | extend Ratio = round(todouble(['Prompt Tokens']) / todouble(['Completion Tokens']), 1)
// | where ['Prompt Tokens'] > 100000
// | order by Ratio desc

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. Reasoning-token overhead and cache hit rate — the gpt-5-class question
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// llm-emit-token-metric emits more than three counters. The full set observed
// on this platform:
//
//     Total Tokens                   Prompt Cached Tokens
//     Prompt Tokens                  Prompt Audio Tokens
//     Completion Tokens              Completion Audio Tokens
//     Completion Reasoning Tokens    Completion Accepted Prediction Tokens
//                                    Completion Rejected Prediction Tokens
//
// Two of those deserve a dashboard tile of their own:
//
//   Completion Reasoning Tokens — billed, invisible to the user, and driven by
//     the reasoning-effort setting. If this is a large fraction of completion
//     tokens on a simple classification workload, the effort level is wrong.
//     This is exactly what the product policy's reasoningEffortDefault exists
//     to control, and this is how you prove the setting was worth having.
//
//   Prompt Cached Tokens — tokens served from the provider's prompt cache at a
//     discount. A rising cached-token share is the measurable payoff from
//     restructuring prompts so the stable part comes first.
//
// AppMetrics
// | where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
// | where Name in ("Completion Tokens", "Completion Reasoning Tokens", "Prompt Cached Tokens", "Prompt Tokens")
// | extend Team = tostring(Properties.Team), Model = tostring(Properties.Model)
// | summarize Tokens = sum(Sum) by Team, Model, Name
// | evaluate pivot(Name, sum(Tokens))
// | extend ReasoningSharePct = round(100.0 * todouble(['Completion Reasoning Tokens']) / todouble(['Completion Tokens']), 1)
// | extend CacheHitSharePct  = round(100.0 * todouble(['Prompt Cached Tokens']) / todouble(['Prompt Tokens']), 1)
// | order by ReasoningSharePct desc

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5. Model mix — is anyone using a frontier model for a trivial job
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// AppMetrics
// | where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
// | where Name == "Total Tokens"
// | extend Team = tostring(Properties.Team), Model = tostring(Properties.Model)
// | summarize Tokens = sum(Sum) by Team, Model
// | order by Tokens desc

// =============================================================================
// A NOTE ON DIMENSION DISCIPLINE
// =============================================================================
// Every product policy emits the SAME six dimensions: CostCenter, Team, App,
// Model, ServiceTier, ApiId. That uniformity is a platform contract, not a
// stylistic preference. The moment one team adds a seventh dimension or renames
// Team to TeamName, every query above needs a special case for that team, and
// six months later the reporting layer is a pile of exceptions nobody trusts.
//
// Enforce it by keeping the emit-metric block identical across product policies
// and reviewing changes to it in a pull request. The values differ per team;
// the KEYS must not.
//
// Two hard limits worth knowing before you settle the dimension set:
//   * a maximum of 10 dimensions per emit-metric policy, and
//   * dimensions are the only axes the report can EVER slice on. Adding one
//     later does not backfill history. Choosing them is a one-way door.
//
// One more scope trap, which we hit live: inbound policies evaluate
// global → product → api → operation, and llm-emit-token-metric is
// inbound-only. A dimension that reads a variable set by the API policy will
// therefore emit an empty value from a PRODUCT-scoped emit block — silently.
// Our Model dimension read "unresolved" on every row until we resolved the
// model inside the product policy itself. Always check the emitted dimensions
// once, in the portal, before you build a dashboard on top of them.
// =============================================================================
